Tracking error rate directly in application code, extending Module 7's centralized error-handling middleware:

app.use((err: Error, req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
  // Reporting EVERY genuine error to a monitoring service, not just
  // logging it locally - this is what makes error RATE genuinely
  // measurable and ALERTABLE in production, rather than only ever
  // visible after the fact in scattered, unaggregated server logs
  monitoringService.reportError(err, { path: req.path, method: req.method });

  res.status(500).json({ success: false, error: 'Internal server error' });
});

A dedicated health-check endpoint, used by external uptime monitors:

app.get('/health', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    await db.command({ ping: 1 }); // genuinely verify the database
                                       // connection is actually working,
                                       // not just that this server
                                       // process happens to be running
    res.status(200).json({ status: 'healthy' });
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(503).json({ status: 'unhealthy' }); // genuinely signal
      // a real problem, distinct from a generic 500 error
  }
});

An alert rule for error rate, directly extending Module 24, lesson 6's
alert-rule structure to a genuinely different metric:

alertRule({
  metric: 'error_rate_5xx',
  threshold: 0.01, // alert if more than 1% of requests are failing
  window: '5m',
  notify: ['#engineering-alerts']
});

