// DELIBERATE TEST: a developer pushes a change with a genuine bug
// that passes all existing tests but causes the application to
// report unhealthy once actually running (a database connection
// string subtly misconfigured for the production environment)

// STAGE 1 (lesson 1's test gate): PASS - existing tests don't
// specifically exercise the actual database connection string value

// STAGE 2 (lesson 1's build gate, lesson 2's container): PASS -
// the Docker image builds successfully; a misconfigured connection
// string doesn't cause a BUILD failure, only a RUNTIME one

// STAGE 3 (lesson 5's deployment, lesson 4's health-check gate):
// the new instance deploys, but lesson 4's health check
// (db.command({ ping: 1 })) FAILS, since the misconfigured
// connection string genuinely cannot reach the real database

// RESULT: lesson 5's wait_for_healthy never succeeds for this
// instance - the rolling deployment correctly ABORTS at this exact
// instance, NEVER adding it back to the load balancer, and lesson 1's
// overall pipeline correctly reports this deployment as FAILED

// CRITICALLY: because this was a ROLLING deployment, the OTHER,
// already-deployed-and-healthy instances continue serving real
// traffic throughout this entire failed attempt - real users
// experience ZERO impact from this caught, blocked bad deployment

